{
  "reason": "Select only model_design because the proposed change controls residual contributions inside the causal NextItNet, while preprocessing, training, and evaluation are frozen. The current model achieves validation HR@10 of 0.625 with its best checkpoint at epoch 10/30; full-strength repeated residual updates motivate testing learned scaling, but are not an established bottleneck. Historical success with four shared blocks supports retaining that arrangement and isolating residual scaling as the sole modification.",
  "module": ["model_design"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": null,
    "model_design": "Add self.residual_scale = nn.Parameter(torch.full((hidden_units,), 0.1)) in NextItNetBlock.__init__, and change its forward return to x + self.residual_scale * self.dropout(y), broadcasting the learned channel scales across batch and sequence dimensions. Retain the current four shared blocks with dilations [1,2,4,8], applied twice through the existing self.num_blocks loop, so each block reuses its scale on both applications; this adds only 256 parameters and tests whether initially smaller convolutional contributions improve generalization on sparse histories. Keep MAXLEN=128, HIDDEN_UNITS=64, NUM_BLOCKS=8, KERNEL_SIZE=3, DROPOUT_RATE=0.2, LR=0.001, WEIGHT_DECAY=0.0, and BATCH_SIZE=128, along with causal convolutions, embeddings, positional embeddings, normalization, activations, masking, initialization, and candidate scoring. Preserve all existing variable names and constructor, forward, encode_sequence, and score_candidates signatures, retain exactly the permitted imports, eight numeric constants, and two classes with methods only, and add no file/network access, printing, training, evaluation, or CSV operations. Consume one of the 44 candidate slots under the unchanged host protocol: training seed 2024, seed42 validation negative sampling, identical splits and 200 validation users with 99 negatives each, BPR loss, Adam, clipping, 30 epochs, checkpoint selection, and score generation; treat an HR@10 increase of 0.01–0.025 as an experimental target rather than an established gain.",
    "training_evaluation": null
  }
}
